WEBVTT

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.500
Sustainable OT security depends on accurate operational practices.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.915
Asset inventory should record hardware, software, firmware, network identity,

00:00:07.915 --> 00:00:12.235
physical location, owner, vendor, function, criticality, communication,

00:00:12.235 --> 00:00:13.655
and support status.

00:00:13.655 --> 00:00:16.610
Inventory changes as equipment is replaced, temporary systems are added,

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.760
and vendors modify support paths.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.115
Configuration management establishes trusted baselines for controllers, network devices,

00:00:23.115 --> 00:00:26.050
servers, workstations, applications, and security controls.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.400
Back up logic and settings, review differences, and document authorized changes.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.880
Change control should define testing, approvals, maintenance windows, rollback,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.560
and post-change validation.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.430
Patch and vulnerability management require coordination.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.395
Monitor vendor advisories, confirm affected assets and versions,

00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:44.760
evaluate exposure and consequence, test updates, and schedule work.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.480
When remediation is delayed, use compensating controls with owners and review dates.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.755
Unsupported technology needs lifecycle planning rather than indefinite exception.

00:00:52.755 --> 00:00:57.280
Logging and monitoring should focus on useful events: remote sessions, authentication,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.780
privilege, configuration transfer, new devices, boundary traffic, security-tool status,

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.865
and changes to expected communication.

00:01:03.865 --> 00:01:07.960
Analysts need operational context to distinguish approved maintenance from suspicious

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:08.760
activity.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.955
Training should prepare operators, engineers, administrators, vendors,

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:14.420
and incident responders for their responsibilities.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.400
Exercises can test loss of visibility, ransomware, remote-access misuse, and recovery.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.651
Lessons should update procedures, architecture, contacts, and control priorities.

00:01:23.651 --> 00:01:27.665
Continuous improvement uses evidence from incidents, near misses, assessments, exceptions,

00:01:27.665 --> 00:01:29.420
maintenance, and technology changes.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.445
Track whether controls work, whether risk decisions remain valid,

00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:34.901
and whether recovery can meet operational needs.

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:38.635
Security becomes part of normal operations when ownership, maintenance, monitoring,

00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:41.040
and engineering decisions reinforce one another.
